import ToMathlib.InformationTheory.Coding.SelfDualGenerator

/-!
# Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.24 — weak self-duality from the generator matrix

*(N&C §10.4.1, p. 450.)*

A code with generator matrix G is weakly self-dual iff G^T G = 0.

## What the exercise asks, and what is proved

A generator matrix `G` encodes a message column vector `x` as the codeword `G *ᵥ x`, so the code it
generates is the range of the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x` (`Matrix.mulVecLin`), i.e. the span of the
columns of `G`. The code is **weakly self-dual** (self-orthogonal, `LinearCode.IsSelfOrthogonal`,
N&C §10.4.1 p. 449) when it is contained in its dual `C ⊆ C⊥`, equivalently when every two codewords
are orthogonal under the standard dot product. The exercise is the equivalence with `Gᵀ G = 0`.

The formalisation is in `ToMathlib/InformationTheory/Coding/SelfDualGenerator.lean`, alongside the
classical-coding development (`LinearCode`, `dual`, `IsSelfOrthogonal`, `GeneratorMatrix`).

## Declarations

In `ToMathlib/InformationTheory/Coding/SelfDualGenerator.lean`, namespace `LinearCode`, over an
arbitrary field `K` (N&C's binary code is `K = ZMod 2`):

* `LinearCode.isSelfOrthogonal_range_mulVecLin_iff` — **the exercise itself**: for a matrix `G`, the
  generated code `range G.mulVecLin` is weakly self-dual iff the Gram matrix `Gᵀ * G = 0`.

## Faithfulness

* **Statement.** N&C's "weakly self-dual iff `Gᵀ G = 0`" is captured verbatim: weak self-duality is
  the library's `LinearCode.IsSelfOrthogonal` (`C ⊆ C⊥`, N&C §10.4.1), the code generated by `G` is
  `LinearMap.range G.mulVecLin` (the column span of `G`, the generator-matrix convention of
  `ToMathlib/InformationTheory/Coding/GeneratorMatrix.lean`), and `Gᵀ G` is the Gram matrix
  `Gᵀ * G`. Nothing is weakened.
* **Generality.** Proved over an arbitrary field `K`, so N&C's binary `K = ZMod 2` is a special
  case. The Gram condition `Gᵀ * G = 0` is genuinely weaker than `G = 0` in characteristic `2`
  (a nonzero column can be self-orthogonal), which is exactly what makes weakly self-dual binary
  codes — the classical ingredient of the CSS quantum codes — nontrivial.
-/
